{"tool_name":"oases","contents":["biocontainers","bioschemas","debian","biotools","bioconda","oeb"],"fetched_metadata":{"bioconda":{"name":"oases","version":"0.2.09","home":"https://github.com/dzerbino/oases","documentation":"https://github.com/dzerbino/oases/blob/0.2.09/README.md","license":"GPL-3.0-or-later","summary":"De novo transcriptome assembler for short reads.","identifiers":["doi:10.1093/bioinformatics/bts094","biotools:oases"],"license_file":"LICENSE.txt","build":{"number":4,"run_exports":[null]},"requirements":{"build":["make",null],"host":["libgomp","llvm-openmp","git-lfs"],"run":["velvet","libgomp","llvm-openmp"]},"test":{"commands":["oases | grep \"oases - De novo transcriptome assembler for the Velvet package\" > /dev/null"]}},"biocontainers":{"name":"oases","identifiers":["biotools:oases","doi:10.1093/bioinformatics/bts094"],"license":"GPL","summary":"De novo transcriptome assembler for short reads","home_url":"https://github.com/dzerbino/oases","keywords":["Alternative Splicing","Animals","Gene Expression Profiling","High-Throughput Nucleotide Sequencing","Humans","Mice","RNA, Messenger","Sequence Analysis, RNA","Transcriptomics","Whole genome sequencing","RNA-seq","Sequence assembly","RNA-Seq","Transcriptome"],"manually_check":true,"total_pulls":1},"biotools":{"id":"oases","home":"https://github.com/dzerbino/oases","license":"GPL-3.0","summary":"De novo transcriptome assembler designed to produce transcripts from short read sequencing technologies. It uploads a preliminary assembly produced by Velvet, and clusters the contigs into small groups, called loci. It then exploits the paired-end read and long read information, when available, to construct transcript isoforms.","addition_date":"2017-01-13T13:15:44Z","last_update_date":"2024-11-25T14:23:04.891097Z","tool_type":["Workflow"],"biotools_curie":"biotools:oases","cost":"Free of charge","credit":[{"email":"dzerbino@soe.ucsc.edu","name":"Daniel Zerbino","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Primary contact"]},{"email":"marcel.schulz@molgen.mpg.de","name":"Marcel Schulz","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Primary contact"]}],"documentation":[{"type":["General"],"url":"https://github.com/dzerbino/oases/blob/master/README.md"}],"edit_permission":{"authors":["ELIXIR-EE"],"type":"group"},"function":[{"input":[{"data":{"term":"Sequence assembly","uri":"http://edamontology.org/data_0925"}}],"note":"After running the previous process for different values of k it is necessary to merge the results of all these assemblies (contained in transcripts.fa files) into a single, non redundant assembly. (outputs of previous function) redicted transcript sequences explains the composition of these transcripts,","operation":[{"term":"Aggregation","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3436"}],"output":[{"data":{"term":"RNA sequence","uri":"http://edamontology.org/data_3495"}},{"data":{"term":"Sequence assembly report","uri":"http://edamontology.org/data_3181"}}]},{"input":[{"data":{"term":"Sequence assembly","uri":"http://edamontology.org/data_0925"}}],"note":"Single assembly (to run Oases it is recommended to run an array of single- k assemblies, for different values of k (i.e. the hash length). These assemblies are then merged into a final assembly.) Each single- k assembly consists of a simple Velvet run, followed by an Oases run.","operation":[{"term":"Transcriptome assembly","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3258"}],"output":[{"data":{"term":"Sequence assembly","uri":"http://edamontology.org/data_0925"}}]}],"maturity":"Mature","name":"Oases","operating_system":["Linux","Mac"],"owner":"seqwiki_import","publication":[{"doi":"10.1093/bioinformatics/bts094","metadata":{"abstract":"Motivation: High-throughput sequencing has made the analysis of new model organisms more affordable. Although assembling a new genome can still be costly and difficult, it is possible to use RNAseq to sequence mRNA. In the absence of a known genome, it is necessary to assemble these sequences de novo, taking into account possible alternative isoforms and the dynamic range of expression values. Results: We present a software package named Oases designed to heuristically assemble RNA-seq reads in the absence of a reference genome, across a broad spectrum of expression values and in presence of alternative isoforms. It achieves this by using an array of hash lengths, a dynamic filtering of noise, a robust resolution of alternative splicing events and the efficient merging of multiple assemblies. It was tested on human and mouse RNA-seq data and is shown to improve significantly on the transABySS and Trinity de novo transcriptome assemblers. \u00a9 The Author(s) 2012. Published by Oxford University Press.","authors":[{"name":"Birney E."},{"name":"Schulz M.H."},{"name":"Vingron M."},{"name":"Zerbino D.R."}],"citationCount":1124,"date":"2012-04-01T00:00:00Z","journal":"Bioinformatics","title":"Oases: Robust de novo RNA-seq assembly across the dynamic range of expression levels"},"pmcid":"PMC3324515","pmid":"22368243"}],"topic":[{"term":"RNA-Seq","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3170"},{"term":"Sequence assembly","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0196"},{"term":"Transcriptomics","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3308"},{"term":"Whole genome sequencing","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3673"}],"validated":1},"bioschemas":{"name":"Oases","home":"https://bio.tools/oases","license":"GPL-3.0","summary":"De novo transcriptome assembler designed to produce transcripts from short read sequencing technologies. It uploads a preliminary assembly produced by Velvet, and clusters the contigs into small groups, called loci. It then exploits the paired-end read and long read information, when available, to construct transcript isoforms.","tool_type":"sc:SoftwareApplication"},"oeb":{"metrics":[{"@id":"https://openebench.bsc.es/monitor/metrics/biotools:oases/workflow/github.com","@license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","@nmsp":"biotools","@type":"me